# Copy to `.env` for local development:  cp .env.example .env
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Which file you put a value in decides whether it stays secret.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
#   .env                 Read by `netlify dev` and handed to the functions in
#                        netlify/functions. Stays on the server. Safe for keys.
#
#   .env.development     Read by webpack (see webpack.config.js), which inlines
#   .env.production      every value into the browser bundle via DefinePlugin.
#                        PUBLIC. Never put a key in these.
#
# webpack only ever loads `.env.<mode>`, never plain `.env`, so a key placed in
# `.env` cannot leak into the bundle by accident. All of these are gitignored.


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# GEMINI_API_KEY — server-side only
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Powers the public demo: with this set, visitors can chat without supplying a
# key of their own. Only netlify/functions/gemini-chat.mts reads it, and it is
# never sent to the browser.
#
#   Local     put it in `.env` and run `netlify dev` (a plain `webpack serve`
#             has no functions, so the demo path is unavailable there)
#   Deployed  Netlify -> Site configuration -> Environment variables.
#             Do NOT add it to .env.production — that publishes it.
#
# Leave it unset and the app still works: the function returns 503 and the UI
# asks each visitor for their own key in Settings, which is stored in their
# browser and used to call Gemini directly.
#
# Get a key from https://aistudio.google.com/apikey
GEMINI_API_KEY=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Client-side variables
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# None. Nothing in src/ reads process.env, and adding a reference to a variable
# that is missing at build time throws at runtime, because `process` does not
# exist in the browser — DefinePlugin only substitutes keys it actually found.
